    "accordion_X9KDBB": {
      "type": "accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "text_8HeDjV": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do you need a water filter for New York tap water?",
            "answer": "<p>The water supply in New York meets federal safety standards, but it can still contain contaminants such as lead, PFAS, and TTHMs. A high-quality water filter can help lower the chance these contaminants will be present in your water supply and can improve the safety and taste of your water.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_QbTBtT": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How to remove lead and PFAS from New York drinking water?",
            "answer": "<p>Gravity or multi-stage filter systems that use advanced filtration technology are designed to effectively reduce lead and PFAS from New York's drinking water supply. These filter systems target both heavy metals and \"forever\" chemicals, and they require very little installation effort.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_XbcVQV": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the best home water filtration system in New York?",
            "answer": "<p>The best water filter system will effectively remove typical local contaminants such as lead, chloramine, and PFAS while being easy to use. Most people prefer gravity-based water filters because they are effective, economical, and do not require plumbing installation.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_NXHddK": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What causes New York contaminated water issues?",
            "answer": "<p>New York's water pollution stems from infrastructure problems, runoff from urban environments, and chemicals used in processing water. Microplastics, pharmaceuticals, and other pollutants can appear in water due to these issues.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_yUeHba": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are PFAS in New York water harmful?",
            "answer": "<p>PFAS (perfluoroalkyl substances) are man-made chemicals that don’t break down in the environment and have persisted in the environment for decades. Long-term consumption of PFAS may result in numerous negative health effects.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_fE9Y4D": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are TTHMs water issues in New York?",
            "answer": "<p>TTHMs are a byproduct of adding disinfectants to water, and they are formed when the disinfectants interact with organic materials found in water. The levels of TTHMs in New York's water are higher in some areas during the summer than at other times, and it can negatively impact both the safety and the taste of the water in those areas.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_XzRedf": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why is chloramine used in New York water?",
            "answer": "<p>The main reason chloramines are added to NY water is because they provide protection for the water by serving as a disinfectant while being transported through pipes to customers. Chloramines can leave a particular flavor in the water therefore, they may need to be filtered to improve their quality. </p>"
          }
        },
        "text_bVMT6E": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is chromium 6 in New York water and should I worry?",
            "answer": "<p>Chromium (VI) is a toxic heavy metal that is found in trace amounts in NY water and is associated with industrial pollution. The greatest worry is for long-term exposure; therefore, any kind of effective filtration is very important. </p>"
          }
        }
      },
